\section{Regex Baseline Details}
\label{sec:regex_baselines}

Five regex baselines are evaluated:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{B1} (``ERROR'' presence): Predicts fail if the string ``ERROR'' appears anywhere in the trace. Accuracy: 93.45\%.
\item \textbf{B2} (``Traceback'' detection): Predicts fail if ``Traceback'' appears. Accuracy: 71.51\% (equivalent to majority baseline---most fails do not produce tracebacks in our format).
\item \textbf{B3} (any error keyword): Union of ERROR, Exception, Error, Traceback. Accuracy: 93.45\%.
\item \textbf{B4} (exception type extraction): Regex matching Python exception class names (e.g., \texttt{TypeError}, \texttt{ValueError}). Accuracy: 93.61\%. McNemar vs.\ SFT: $p{<}0.001$.
\item \textbf{B5} (format marker): Detects \texttt{[No exception data in trace]} (inserted by preprocessing for passing tests). Accuracy: 99.33\%. McNemar vs.\ SFT: $p{=}0.041$.
\end{itemize}

